{%- comment -%}
  The order a metal option's values are shown in.

  The design orders its swatches by its own metal table, not by the order the
  option happens to carry: `metals()` ends `return ORDER.filter(...)`, and ORDER
  is white, yellow, rose, mixed, platinum, palladium, sterling, fine silver.
  Shopify's order is whatever the merchant typed, which is why a card was
  opening on Yellow Gold with White Gold second.

  Anything matching no metal is kept and appended, so an unrecognised value
  stays pickable rather than vanishing from the row.

  Lives here because the card and the quick view both need it and it is the
  design's table, not either component's — two copies would drift the moment a
  metal was added to it.

  A snippet cannot hand a value back, so this *prints* the ordered values joined
  with `||`. Callers capture and split:

    capture raw
      render 'metal-order', option: metal_option
    endcapture
    assign ordered = raw | strip | split: '||'

  Note what comes back through `split` is a plain string, not the
  `product_option_value` drop — the drop is what carries a merchant's native
  swatch, so a caller that paints one has to look the value up again.

  Accepts:
    option - the metal product_option drop. Blank prints nothing.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign ordered_metals = ''

  if option != blank
    assign metal_needles = 'white,yellow,rose,mixed,platinum,palladium,sterling,fine silver' | split: ','

    comment
      The membership test is not optional. A value naming two metals — "White &
      Rose Gold", or the design's own Mixed Metal — matches more than one
      needle, and without it the same value is appended once per match: two
      swatches for one metal, both marked selected, an inflated "+N", and a
      genuine metal pushed past the third slot and hidden.

      The break only fires on an append, so a needle whose first match was
      already placed keeps looking for another value of its own.
    endcomment
    for needle in metal_needles
      for value in option.values
        assign value_down = value | downcase | remove_first: 'test ' | strip
        if value_down contains needle
          assign probe = '||' | append: value | append: '||'
          assign haystack = '||' | append: ordered_metals
          unless haystack contains probe
            assign ordered_metals = ordered_metals | append: value | append: '||'
            break
          endunless
        endif
      endfor
    endfor

    for value in option.values
      assign probe = '||' | append: value | append: '||'
      assign haystack = '||' | append: ordered_metals
      unless haystack contains probe
        assign ordered_metals = ordered_metals | append: value | append: '||'
      endunless
    endfor
  endif
-%}
{{- ordered_metals -}}
